        "heading": "Continuous Monitoring and Early Warning",
        "body": "Traditional shrinkage measurement through periodic physical counts — typically conducted annually or semi-annually — provides only retrospective assessment. By the time a count reveals elevated shrinkage, months of losses may have accumulated. Continuous monitoring using statistical process control (SPC) methods enables much earlier detection. CUSUM (Cumulative Sum) charts applied to the running discrepancy between POS (nidaamka POS)-expected and system-recorded inventory for each SKU detect gradual shifts in shrinkage rates — the pattern characteristic of ongoing employee theft that might add a few units per week. EWMA (Exponentially Weighted Moving Average) charts provide sensitivity to both gradual and sudden shifts. Establishing control limits requires baseline shrinkage rate estimation, which can be derived from historical physical count data or from observed discrepancy distributions during a trusted period. SKUs whose cumulative discrepancy crosses the control limit trigger investigation alerts. The sensitivity-specificity tradeoff is managed through the control limit parameters: tighter limits catch smaller losses sooner but generate more false alarms. For practical implementation, a tiered alerting system — with different thresholds for investigation versus immediate action — balances responsiveness with operational feasibility. askbiz.co implements continuous shrinkage monitoring with configurable alert thresholds, enabling retailers to detect emerging loss patterns without waiting for physical inventory counts."
